import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'gap', label: 'Where watch mode stops working' },
  { id: 'fixture', label: 'Reset the project once per run' },
  { id: 'test', label: 'Write a test against the real client' },
  { id: 'watch', label: 'Start the watcher' },
  { id: 'habits', label: 'Habits that keep the loop fast' },
];
const conftest = `import os
import urllib.request

import pytest


@pytest.fixture(scope="session", autouse=True)
def clean_localcloud_project():
    """Start every watch-mode run from the seeded baseline."""
    gateway = os.environ["LOCALCLOUD_GATEWAY"]
    project = os.environ["GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT"]
    request = urllib.request.Request(
        f"{gateway}/reset?project={project}",
        data=b'{"restore_seed":true}',
        headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"},
        method="POST",
    )
    with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=10) as response:
        assert response.status == 200`;
const testFile = String.raw`import os

from google.auth.credentials import AnonymousCredentials
from google.cloud import storage


def test_export_lands_in_bucket():
    client = storage.Client(
        project=os.environ["GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT"],
        credentials=AnonymousCredentials(),
    )
    bucket = client.create_bucket("exports")
    bucket.blob("daily.csv").upload_from_string("id,total\n1,42\n")
    assert bucket.blob("daily.csv").download_as_text().startswith("id,total")`;
const watchCmd = `localcloud start --local-only
eval "$(localcloud env)"

pip install pytest pytest-watcher google-cloud-storage
ptw tests/`;
const output = `.                                                                        [100%]
1 passed in 1.66s`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="tdd-google-cloud-watch-mode"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="Watch mode is how most of us write unit tests: save, see green or red, keep going. It usually stops at the first line that touches a bucket or a queue. With the cloud on localhost and a reset before each run, it doesn't have to."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/sdk-examples/', label: 'SDK examples' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/docs/', label: 'Quick start' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="A pytest watcher that reruns your cloud integration tests on every save, each run starting from a clean project."
  needs="Python with pytest, pytest-watcher and the Google Cloud client your code uses"
  next={{ posts: ['one-second-cloud-state-resets', 'zero-secret-gcp-ci-github-actions'], doc: { href: '/docs/sdk-examples/', label: 'SDK examples for Storage, Pub/Sub, BigQuery, Bigtable, Spanner and Firestore' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="gap">Where watch mode stops working</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Red, green, refactor depends on the test run being quicker than your attention span. Against a live project it isn't: each save pays for network round trips, leaves buckets and topics behind for the next run to trip over, and puts a small charge on someone's bill.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">So cloud code gets one of two substitutes. Either the client is mocked, and the test checks that you called the mock the way you told the mock to expect. Or the integration tests move to CI, and the feedback arrives ten minutes after you have moved on.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fixture">Reset the project once per run</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">LocalCloud answers SDK calls in a few milliseconds, which solves speed. Leftover state is solved by the reset endpoint: one request clears the project and reapplies your seed data while the container stays up. Put it in a session fixture so every run of the watcher starts clean.</p>
    <BlogCode label="tests/conftest.py" code={conftest} copy="copy-blog-tdd-fixture" />
    <p class="mt-4">Once per run is the right grain. The reset takes a few hundred milliseconds, which is nothing once and noticeable fifty times. Jest and Vitest users can make the same request from <code>globalSetup</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="test">Write a test against the real client</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">No mocks and no special test client. This is the same <code>google-cloud-storage</code> code path your application uses, pointed at localhost by the exported environment.</p>
    <BlogCode label="tests/test_exports.py" code={testFile} copy="copy-blog-tdd-test" />
    <p class="mt-4">Notice that the test creates a bucket with a fixed name. Against a shared project that fails on the second run. Here the fixture has already cleared it.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="watch">Start the watcher</h2>
    <BlogCode label="Watch-mode commands" code={watchCmd} copy="copy-blog-tdd-watch" />
    <BlogCode label="Output after each save" code={output} />
    <p class="mt-4">Save a file and the suite reruns. A second and a half for a reset, a bucket, an upload and a download is quick enough to stay in the loop, and the run after it starts from the same clean project.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="habits">Habits that keep the loop fast</h2>
    <ul class="site-list mt-5">
      <li><strong>Watch a narrow path.</strong> Point the watcher at the tests for the module you are changing, and run the full suite before you push.</li>
      <li><strong>Seed what every test needs.</strong> Shared topics and tables belong in the <a href="/docs/seed-data/">seed file</a>, so tests only create what is specific to them.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the same tests in CI.</strong> The suite that runs on save runs unchanged in <a href="/docs/github-actions/">GitHub Actions</a>, so green locally means green on the pull request.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
